const faq: FaqItem[] = [
	{
		q: 'Is TBMQ a drop-in replacement for Mosquitto?',
		a: 'At the protocol level, yes — TBMQ speaks MQTT 3.1, 3.1.1 and 5.0, so your clients need no code changes. Some may need new settings: TBMQ refuses anonymous clients and closes the connection of any client that sends a packet over 64 KB by default, and has no <code>$SYS/broker</code> tree. It also installs a password-less <code>tbmq_websockets_username</code> credential for its built-in WebSocket client — set a password on it before going live. Operationally, no: TBMQ runs with Kafka, Valkey and PostgreSQL alongside it, which is a different deployment shape than a single binary.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I run TBMQ on a Raspberry Pi like Mosquitto?',
		a: "It isn't what TBMQ is built for: the broker plus Kafka, Valkey and PostgreSQL need at least 2 GB of RAM, so a small Pi or a gateway is the wrong home for it. The usual pattern is a lightweight broker on the device or gateway bridging into a central TBMQ cluster — one Mosquitto bridge in MQTT 5 mode carries that traffic in both directions.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Does Mosquitto support clustering?',
		a: 'Open-source Mosquitto does not. Bridging forwards messages between brokers, but it shares no session or subscription state — so a bridged pair behaves as two brokers, not one cluster, and a session cannot resume on the other node.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How do I migrate from Mosquitto without downtime?',
		a: 'Bridge the two brokers with one Mosquitto bridge in MQTT 5 mode that carries your topics in both directions, then repoint clients in batches and remove the bridge. No flag day and no maintenance window.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Is TBMQ free?',
		a: 'TBMQ Community Edition is open source under the Apache 2.0 license, including commercial use. Professional Edition adds single sign-on, role-based access control, audit logs and white labeling.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How many concurrent connections can Mosquitto handle?',
		// 8,192: Mosquitto's own README-windows.txt, the only capacity figure the project documents.
		a: 'The project publishes no benchmark of its own — the 2.0 announcement said figures were planned and none followed, and the only capacity number the project documents is a Windows platform ceiling of roughly 8,192 connections. The real ceiling depends on file descriptors, memory and the fact that message handling runs on a single thread. <strong>The useful answer is that throughput is limited to one CPU core on one machine</strong> — which is why the decision to move is usually about clustering and durability rather than a specific number.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does Mosquitto have a web UI?',
		a: "Since 2.1 it does — a dashboard over the <code>$SYS</code> counters, served by an <code>http_api</code> listener that the manual calls “a very simple webserver … that can also serve some HTTP API requests” — three read-only endpoints, for the version, the listeners and the <code>$SYS</code> counters. It reports totals and rates: how many clients are connected, how much traffic moved, how many messages are retained, how long the broker has been up. It is <strong>read-only</strong>, and it has no view of <em>which</em> — no client list, no subscription list, no topic browser. Through an API, runtime change is limited to authentication and authorization over <code>$CONTROL</code> topics; anything else is a config edit plus a reload signal or a restart. TBMQ's UI and REST API create and edit credentials, authorization rules, integrations and shared subscriptions on a running cluster.",
	},
	{
		q: 'Can I run TBMQ on a single node?',
		a: 'Yes, and it is how most people start. The standard Docker install runs one TBMQ node with Kafka, Valkey and PostgreSQL beside it, plus the Integration Executor that runs integrations, in at least 2 GB of RAM. That gives you the durability guarantee and the separate device and application paths without a cluster; you add nodes when you need the headroom or the availability. With a single Kafka broker, though, an acknowledged message survives a process crash, not the loss of the host. What a single node does not give you is a single binary — that is the trade this page is about.',
	},
];
---

<MqttTopicLayout slug="mosquitto-alternative" faq={faq}>
	<h2>What Mosquitto is built for</h2>
	<p>
		Mosquitto is a single lightweight binary that runs on a router, a single-board computer or a spare VM, and it
		implements MQTT 5.0, shared subscriptions included. That combination is what makes it the usual choice for a first
		deployment — and it is a design that commits, deliberately, to one process on one host.
	</p>
	<p>
		That commitment is the whole story of this page. What Mosquitto does well follows from staying small, and what it
		cannot do follows from the same decision. So the question is not whether it is a good broker — it is whether your
		deployment has outgrown a single one.
	</p>
	<p>
		One scoping note: <strong>Mosquitto here means the open-source Eclipse project</strong>. Cedalo's commercial Pro
		Edition is a separate product with its own feature set and licence, and is not what the sections below compare.
	</p>

	<h2>The three walls</h2>
	<p>
		Deployments outgrow Mosquitto in three specific ways. They are worth naming precisely, because only the first one is
		really about scale.
	</p>

	<h3>The cluster wall</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto runs as a single broker. It can bridge to another broker, and a bridge does forward messages — but it
		shares no session state and no subscription state. Two bridged brokers are two brokers, not one logical broker: a
		client's session lives on the node it connected to, and if that node is gone, the session is gone with it. Within
		one broker, vertical scaling is the only headroom you have, and less of it than it looks: all message handling runs
		on a single thread, so a bigger machine buys memory for more connections, not cores for more throughput. Splitting
		clients across independent brokers adds capacity when traffic partitions cleanly — but that is several brokers, not
		one.
	</p>

	<h3>The durability wall</h3>
	{
		/* 5s = persist-sqlite plugin_opt_flush_period default; 30min = autosave_interval default. Both Mosquitto 2.1.2 defaults.
		   The plugin runs WAL with plugin_opt_sync normal by default, so a commit can roll back on power loss; since 2.1.2
		   the broker refuses `persistence true` together with a persistence plugin. */
	}
	<p>
		When Mosquitto returns a PUBACK, the message is in that process's memory — and at the defaults that is the only
		place it ever is: persistence is off, so even a clean restart comes back empty. Enable persistence and queued and
		retained messages reach disk after the acknowledgement — with the built-in <code>persistence true</code>, at the
		next autosave, thirty minutes by default; with 2.1's SQLite persistence plugin (used instead of
		<code>persistence true</code>), at the next commit, within five seconds, though at its default
		<code>sync normal</code> a power cut can still roll back the latest commits. Both also write on a clean shutdown, so
		a crash or a power cut takes whatever changed since the last durable write: queued messages, retained messages and
		session state. The acknowledgement your publisher already holds is therefore a promise about one process on one
		machine.
	</p>

	<h3>The mixed-traffic wall</h3>
	<p>
		This is the one nobody markets against, and it is usually what actually hurts. IoT traffic is not one kind of
		traffic. Thousands of devices publish telemetry continuously. A handful of backend applications subscribe to
		high-volume streams and must not miss anything. Commands target one specific client and have to arrive even when
		that client is briefly offline. Mosquitto gives all three the same treatment: one queue policy for every session,
		held in memory. A command for a device that is briefly offline waits in that same memory (not at all at QoS 0) and,
		at the defaults, does not survive a restart. A backend consumer that falls behind does not slow the devices down —
		once 1,000 messages are queued for it, the broker drops the rest, while each publisher still gets its PUBACK. The
		client that must not miss anything is the one that loses messages.
	</p>

	<h2>How TBMQ answers each one</h2>
	<p>
		TBMQ is built on <a href="https://kafka.apache.org/">Kafka</a> for durability and distribution,
		<a href="https://netty.io/">Netty</a> for non-blocking transport, and an actor per client for concurrency. Each of the
		three sections below answers one wall.
	</p>

	<h3>Acknowledged means stored</h3>
	<p>
		TBMQ does not send a PUBACK or PUBREC until Kafka has confirmed the write; if Kafka cannot take it, the publisher
		never gets a success — an MQTT 5 client receives an error reason code, and an MQTT 3.1.1 client is disconnected.
		Once the publisher holds that acknowledgement, the message no longer depends on the node that received it: if that
		node dies, another picks up from Kafka and continues delivery. Being precise about the limit — the shipped defaults
		keep a single copy (<code>acks=1</code>, replication factor <code>1</code>), so surviving the loss of a Kafka broker
		means running a replicated Kafka with matching producer acknowledgements. The guarantee is real; how far it extends
		is something you configure.
	</p>

	<h3>One cluster, no coordinator</h3>
	<p>
		Every TBMQ node is identical. There is no master and no leader election, so no broker node becomes a bottleneck or a
		single point of failure — Kafka, Valkey and PostgreSQL need their own replication for that to hold end to end.
		Persistent-session and subscription state is shared through Kafka, so when a persistent client reconnects after a
		node failure, any node in the cluster can resume its session. Adding a node needs no resharding and no downtime:
		consumer groups rebalance and load spreads across the larger cluster. At the defaults the shared message topic has
		16 partitions and each node runs four consumers on it, so past four nodes, raise the partition count as well.
	</p>
	<p>
		Identical nodes are also what keeps the deployment ordinary. TBMQ ships an official
		<a href="/docs/installation/?installationType=helm">Helm chart</a>, with setup guides for EKS, AKS, GKE and
		minikube, and every node exposes a <a href="/docs/other/health/">health endpoint</a> — so draining and replacing one is
		a Kubernetes rollout rather than a broker-specific procedure. No node has to go last.
	</p>

	<h3>Device and application traffic are delivered separately</h3>
	<p>
		TBMQ splits persistent clients into two types, which you assign to match how each one behaves. <strong
			>DEVICE</strong
		> clients publish often and subscribe to little; while one is offline its messages wait in a per-client queue in Valkey
		or Redis — up to 10,000 messages for seven days by default, oldest dropped first — which absorbs high write rates and
		hands the queue back quickly on reconnect.
		<strong>APPLICATION</strong> clients subscribe to high-volume topics, and each one gets a dedicated Kafka topic and its
		own consumer, so a backlog accumulates safely in Kafka and is replayed in order from that client's committed offset, kept
		for up to seven days or about 1 GB, whichever comes first, by default. A slow application subscriber's backlog stays in
		its own topic, so it cannot hold up delivery to devices. Every publish still enters through one shared Kafka topic, though,
		so a large enough device burst delays everything behind it, on TBMQ as on any broker.
	</p>

