	<h2>What else you get</h2>
	<p>Two things that are not answers to a wall, and one licensing note.</p>

	<h3>Shared subscriptions that add throughput</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto implements MQTT 5.0 shared subscriptions and so does TBMQ, so this is not a support difference — it is a
		difference in what adding a member does. Register an
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/shared-subscriptions/">application shared subscription</a> and the group gets its own Kafka
		topic; each subscribing APPLICATION client joins that topic's consumer group, so an extra instance picks up a share of
		the partitions and the group rebalances when one dies. Messages keep accumulating in Kafka while every member is offline,
		and delivery resumes from the committed offset.
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/shared-subscriptions/">Shared subscriptions</a> covers the DEVICE-client behavior and the load-balancing
		strategies.
	</p>

	<h3>You can change it while it runs, not just watch it</h3>
	<p>
		Mosquitto 2.1 added a dashboard over the <code>$SYS</code> counters, so seeing broker state is no longer the gap it was.
		What it reports is totals and rates — how many clients are connected, how much traffic moved, how many messages are retained.
		What it cannot tell you is <em>which</em>: there is no client list, no subscription list, no topic browser. And it
		changes nothing, because it is read-only.
	</p>
	<p>
		Configuration is a file, and a reload signal does re-read much of it, bridges included — but it cannot add or remove
		a listener, and it cannot load a plugin or change a plugin's options. A plugin that is already loaded does re-read
		its own password or ACL file. In 2.1 the plugin limit reaches further than it sounds, because plugins are now the
		recommended route for authentication, authorization and persistence. Adding the password-file plugin to a running
		broker is a restart, not a reload: ask for it with a reload and <code>allow_anonymous false</code> takes effect while
		the plugin holding the passwords does not, which locks out everyone until the restart. (The deprecated
		<code>password_file</code> option still reloads.) Runtime change is limited to authentication and authorization, driven
		over <code>$CONTROL</code> topics with <code>mosquitto_ctrl</code> and the dynamic-security plugin; 2.1's broker control
		API is off by default and only lists plugins and listeners.
	</p>
	<p>
		In TBMQ those are entities, not file entries. <a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/mqtt-client-credentials/">Credentials</a> and
		authorization rules, <a href="/docs/integrations/">integrations</a>,
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/shared-subscriptions/">shared subscriptions</a>, blocked clients and
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/users/">users</a> are created and edited while the cluster runs, from the
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/monitoring/">web UI</a> or the <a href="/docs/rest-api/">REST API</a>, with no restart
		and no file to ship to every node. The same views answer the operational questions — which
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/sessions/">sessions</a> are connected and what each is subscribed to, which connection attempts
		were rejected and why — plus a built-in
		<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/websocket-client/">WebSocket client</a> for publishing a test message without leaving the
		browser, and metrics export to <a href="/docs/prometheus-metrics/">Prometheus</a>. Broker-level tuning is a config
		file in both: Mosquitto reloads part of it on a signal, and TBMQ applies it on a restart.
	</p>

	<h3>Open source, and what Professional Edition adds</h3>
	<p>
		Everything above is Community Edition, <a href="https://github.com/thingsboard/tbmq">Apache 2.0</a>, commercial use
		included. <a href="/pricing/">Professional Edition</a> adds OAuth 2.0 single sign-on, role-based access control, audit
		logs, white labeling and a <a href="/docs/pe/user-guide/dropped-messages/">dropped messages</a> view that records
		every message the broker permanently lost, with the reason, client and topic — on the same broker, not a different
		one.
	</p>

	<h2>What the architecture is built to do</h2>
	<p>
		Each figure comes from a published TBMQ test, with its hardware and load profile documented.
	</p>

