	<p>
		Every Mosquitto column above describes the open-source Eclipse broker at its defaults, unless the cell says
		otherwise. Several of these gaps are things Cedalo's commercial Pro Edition fills; none of them are things
		open-source Mosquitto claims to do.
	</p>

	<h2 id="stay">Where Mosquitto still fits</h2>
	<p>
		Three cases where the move is not worth making yet. If your deployment is a few thousand clients on one host with no
		availability requirement beyond restarting a service, TBMQ's Kafka, Valkey and PostgreSQL dependencies cost you
		operational surface and buy you nothing. If the hardware is a gateway or an edge box with a few hundred megabytes of
		RAM, TBMQ is not built for that envelope — though a small broker there can still bridge into a central TBMQ cluster,
		which is the usual shape once there is more than one site. And if you want the protocol and nothing else — no
		cluster, no admin API, no integrations — the extra moving parts are a cost rather than a feature.
	</p>

	<h2>Moving over without a flag day</h2>
	<p>
		You do not have to cut over at once. One Mosquitto bridge in MQTT 5 mode carries the bridged topics in both
		directions, so clients on either broker see each other's messages while you move them across in batches.
	</p>

	<MqttDiagram caption="One bridge, one connection, both directions, for as long as the migration takes.">
		<svg
