	<h3>Point one Mosquitto bridge at TBMQ</h3>
	<p>
		Add a bridge connection to Mosquitto with <code>bridge_protocol_version mqttv50</code>,
		<code>notifications false</code> and a <code>topic … both</code> line for each topic tree you are moving, and give it
		its own APPLICATION credentials in TBMQ. Every existing client keeps its current endpoint and configuration.
	</p>

	<h3>Let the same bridge carry traffic back</h3>
	<p>
		Messages published on TBMQ return over the same connection, so consumers that still read from Mosquitto keep working
		with no coordinated change. In MQTT 5 mode the bridge sets the No Local flag on its own subscription to TBMQ, so it
		never receives its own messages back — an MQTT 3.1.1 bridge in both directions delivers each message twice, and
		adding TBMQ's MQTT Integration as a second leg in the opposite direction loops it endlessly.
	</p>

	<h3>Repoint clients in batches, then remove the bridge</h3>
	<p>
		Devices first, backend consumers last, and no maintenance window at any point. Sessions do not cross the bridge: a
		repointed client starts a fresh session on TBMQ, and anything still queued for it on Mosquitto stays there, so move
		persistent clients while they are connected and caught up.
	</p>

	<h3>The parts that are easy to forget</h3>
	<p>Seven things that are not in the diagram and are the usual source of surprises.</p>

	<LearnIconRows>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:users-group" title="Set a client type per credential">
			TBMQ routes persistent clients by <a href="/docs/user-guide/mqtt-client-type/">client type</a>, so a backend
			consumer left as DEVICE gets a per-client queue instead of its own Kafka topic. Decide this before you repoint,
			not after.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:key" title="Re-provision credentials">
			A Mosquitto password file and ACL file do not carry over: the file holds password hashes, and TBMQ needs the
			passwords themselves. Create the matching
			<a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/mqtt-client-credentials/">client credentials</a> and authorization rules in TBMQ first
			— <a href="/docs/other/bulk-provisioning/">bulk provisioning</a> takes a file rather than one form per device.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:route" title="Change two bridge defaults">
			Mosquitto&apos;s default bridge never gets past CONNECT with TBMQ. With notifications on, it registers a Will
			under
			<code>$SYS/broker/connection/</code>, and TBMQ refuses any client whose Will topic starts with <code>$</code>, so
			set
			<code>notifications false</code>. An MQTT 3.1.1 bridge also needs <code>try_private false</code>; in MQTT 5 mode
			that setting no longer matters.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:settings" title="Check TBMQ's defaults against your clients">
			TBMQ refuses anonymous clients, closes the connection of any client that sends a packet over 64 KB, starts with
			its TLS listener off, and publishes no
			<code>$SYS/broker/</code> tree — monitoring that reads it moves to TBMQ&apos;s UI or
			<a href="/docs/prometheus-metrics/">Prometheus</a> metrics. Each is a setting to change or a client to adjust before
			its batch. One more: the password-less <code>tbmq_websockets_username</code> credential TBMQ installs for its built-in
			WebSocket client admits any client that sends that username — set a password on it first.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:pin" title="Check retained messages after the bridge comes up">
			When the bridge connects, each broker&apos;s retained messages on the bridged topics reach the other, and a
			cleared retained message is cleared on both. Retained topics outside the bridged filters stay where they are —
			re-publish the ones that matter on TBMQ.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:list-check" title="Verify each batch before the next">
			After repointing a batch, confirm in TBMQ's <a href="/docs/user-guide/ui/sessions/">sessions view</a> that every client
			is connected with the subscriptions you expect. A silently missing subscription is the failure mode worth catching early.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:arrow-back-up" title="Keep the rollback until the end">
			Leave the bridge up until the last batch has run clean. Until it comes down, reverting a batch is repointing it
			back — no data movement and no restore, although messages TBMQ already queued for a persistent client stay in
			TBMQ.
		</LearnIconRow>
	</LearnIconRows>

